WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.859 --> 00:00:05.810
There are two Republican Senate races for
2020 that we've been paying very close attention

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:09.280
to and in both of those races.

00:00:09.280 --> 00:00:12.900
Republican incumbents are in bigger and bigger
trouble every single week.

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.030
And it is delightful news.

00:00:15.030 --> 00:00:20.240
It's there's so much bad news going around
right now, bad economic news, bad health news,

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:24.100
bad news about, you know, the laughable nature
of American leadership.

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:28.790
Global, the globe, the presidency is seen
as a global laughing stock around the world.

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:30.340
This is good news.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.070
I'm talking about Arizona and Maine.

00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:36.980
I'll tell you about both of them, starting
with Arizona and Arizona.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.800
Martha McSally is the junior senator.

00:00:39.800 --> 00:00:40.980
She has never been elected.

00:00:40.980 --> 00:00:45.710
She was appointed to serve out the remainder
of the late Republican Senator John McCain's

00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:46.710
seat.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:47.710
She's horrible.

00:00:47.710 --> 00:00:49.610
I mean, truly horrible.

00:00:49.610 --> 00:00:52.750
She now wants to be elected for a full term.

00:00:52.750 --> 00:00:58.050
She's running against or running against her,
rather, is astronaut Mark Kelly.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.899
Mark Kelly is the brother of astronaut Scott
Kelly.

00:01:00.899 --> 00:01:02.350
Scott's been a guest on this program.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.030
Very interesting conversation.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:09.130
Mark Kelly is also the husband of former Democratic
Congresswoman Gabby Giffords, who was tragically

00:01:09.130 --> 00:01:10.370
shot years ago.

00:01:10.370 --> 00:01:12.080
You may remember the story.

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:17.130
And Mark Kelly would be a tremendously good
replacement for Martha McSally.

00:01:17.130 --> 00:01:19.320
And McSally isn't doing well.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.650
And I take great pleasure in that.

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:27.050
In the latest poll, Mark Kelly is ahead of
Martha McSally by nine points.

00:01:27.050 --> 00:01:28.570
This is huge.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:33.110
It's hugely important for the 20/20 balance
of Senate power, which we've been talking

00:01:33.110 --> 00:01:38.640
about, because if Democrats are going to take
over control of the Senate in November and

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:44.220
it's a very uphill battle, winning this seat
back is essentially a necessity.

00:01:44.220 --> 00:01:47.080
Then we go to the state of Maine in Maine.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:53.960
We have our old friend, the perennially disappointed
Republican Senator Susan Collins.

00:01:53.960 --> 00:01:55.810
She has a serious opponent.

00:01:55.810 --> 00:01:59.080
And that opponent is Democrat Sarah Gideon.

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:02.210
Susan Collins was first elected in 1996.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:07.939
She's had pretty easy reelections every six
years in 2002 and 2008.

00:02:07.939 --> 00:02:08.939
And in 2014.

00:02:08.939 --> 00:02:14.230
And she is sort of known for this is not a
great thing to be known for, but she's known

00:02:14.230 --> 00:02:19.860
for acting like she might take a stand against
her own party, against the Republican Party.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.840
But then in the end, she always falls in line.

00:02:21.840 --> 00:02:28.030
You might remember that she was very disappointed
with Brett Kavanaugh, but then voted to confirm

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:29.819
him to the Supreme Court.

00:02:29.819 --> 00:02:32.660
She was very disappointed with Donald.

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:37.510
Trump's actions with Ukraine, but voted to
acquit him in Trump's impeachment trial months

00:02:37.510 --> 00:02:39.860
ago, even though it feels like that was years
ago.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:44.430
So it is very much long overdue for Susan
Collins to go.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:45.430
Bye-Bye.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:47.090
Sarah Gideon is trying.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:48.700
The latest poll is old now.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:52.970
It's from March, but it has Gideon four points
ahead, very tight.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.890
But heading into her last reelection, Susan
Collins had pretty high approval ratings,

00:02:56.890 --> 00:03:00.150
as high as 69 percent in 2014.

00:03:00.150 --> 00:03:04.250
Susan Collins latest approval rating is 37
percent.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:09.880
OK, Susan Collins was reelected in 2014 with
a 69 percent approval rating.

00:03:09.880 --> 00:03:12.730
Her approval rating is now 39 percent.

00:03:12.730 --> 00:03:14.950
This is very bad news for Susan Collins.

00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.870
It's very good news for Sarah Gideon.

00:03:16.870 --> 00:03:21.120
But both of these races races are going to
be fights, so we can't take anything for granted.

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.780
The reason I'm telling you this is not so
we can all sit back and say, hey, we've got

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.769
two of these that we're going to take over.

00:03:26.769 --> 00:03:32.920
Why I'm telling you this is to get you involved
as you think about phone banking if we remain

00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:35.650
stuck at home for the foreseeable future.

00:03:35.650 --> 00:03:38.260
These are two great races where you could
get involved.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.031
Now, I know some of you are going to write
to me and you're going to say, David, you

00:03:42.031 --> 00:03:44.170
centrists shill.

00:03:44.170 --> 00:03:50.209
Neither Sarah Gideon nor Mark Kelly are uber
progressive Bernie Sanders types.

00:03:50.209 --> 00:03:51.849
That's true.

00:03:51.849 --> 00:03:56.830
But we're talking about removing some really
toxic Republicans here.

00:03:56.830 --> 00:03:58.849
We should all be fighting hard for that.

00:03:58.849 --> 00:04:04.370
Neither Mark Kelly nor Sara Gideon are as
left as Bernie Sanders, but they are orders

00:04:04.370 --> 00:04:07.220
of magnitude better than what we have there
right now.

00:04:07.220 --> 00:04:09.650
And it's going to be either McSally or Kelly.

00:04:09.650 --> 00:04:10.650
Right.

00:04:10.650 --> 00:04:11.650
That's it.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:13.400
It's either going to be Susan Collins or Sarah
Gideon.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:14.940
Those are the choices.

00:04:14.940 --> 00:04:18.389
And defeating these two would be plus two
for Democrats in the Senate.

00:04:18.389 --> 00:04:21.389
We're going to lose Alabama right now.

00:04:21.389 --> 00:04:25.479
Former senator and former Attorney General
Jeff Sessions is running against Doug Jones.

00:04:25.479 --> 00:04:33.199
Remember, Doug Jones won against in a special
election against a serial accused sex assaulter

00:04:33.199 --> 00:04:34.199
predator.

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:37.119
I don't know what word you want to apply to
him.

00:04:37.119 --> 00:04:39.379
And now Jeff Sessions wants his seat back.

00:04:39.379 --> 00:04:42.409
Jeff Sessions is winning by 13 in the latest
polls.

00:04:42.409 --> 00:04:44.639
We will almost certainly lose that seat.

00:04:44.639 --> 00:04:49.270
So we need these two just to get plus one
very important.

00:04:49.270 --> 00:04:50.539
Consider phone banking there.

00:04:50.539 --> 00:04:53.280
We'll talk about other important races upcoming.

